@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: why a host OAuth credential cannot be provisioned into sbx, and the one remedy that can
' covers: internal/sbx/run.go (AuthLoginArgs)
' covers: internal/credentials/keychain.go (NeedsSandboxLogin, SandboxLoginHint)
' covers: internal/run/run.go (reportSandboxLogin, selectBackend)
'   what proveo can read ........ _spec/internal/secretref/secret-references.puml
'   the credential path it joins . _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the exposure it does NOT close _spec/_plans/secret-custody.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── UPSTREAM, VERBATIM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/security/credentials/ (read 2026-09-03):
'   "there is no supported way to import an existing OAuth token"
'   "You sign in on the host, and the proxy handles token refresh and routing."
'   "Proxy-managed OAuth isn't supported for third-party sandbox agents,
'    including kits that extend a built-in agent."
' `sbx secret import --help` names the producer: services "that already have an
' OAuth token configured (e.g. anthropic after `sbx run claude … -- auth login`)
' are skipped".
'
' So the oauth slot has exactly one writer, and it is sbx itself, capturing an
' in-sandbox sign-in at its proxy. proveo is a third-party kit extending a
' built-in agent — the sentence above names that case directly.
'
' ── WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE macOS KEYCHAIN ─────────────────────────────────
' proveo CAN read the host Keychain, and does, on every darwin run. What it
' reads is the subscription OAuth blob — the whole credential, access and
' refresh tokens with both stamps. That is the right unit and the wrong
' destination: there is no slot to put it in.
'
' Measured on this host: no API-key item exists either. `anthropic-api`,
' `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and `anthropic` all return exit 44, "The specified item
' could not be found in the keychain". So the Keychain holds exactly one
' credential and sbx accepts exactly the other kind.
'
' The remedy is therefore not a better resolver. It is a sign-in performed
' where sbx can see it, after which the token lives in sbx's own store — which
' on darwin IS the login Keychain, service group com.docker.sandboxes. The
' credential ends up in the same keychain either way; what differs is who owns
' the entry, and only sbx's own entry is wired to its proxy.
'
' ── WHY proveo RENDERS THE ARGV AND DOES NOT RUN IT ────────────────────────
' The sign-in needs a browser and a terminal. A run that launched the flow on
' its own would hang waiting for a person who may not be there, and unattended
' runs are the ones that most need a clear failure. So AuthLoginArgs builds the
' command, NeedsSandboxLogin decides whether saying anything is warranted, and
' the operator runs it once.
'
' NeedsSandboxLogin is a stack of reasons to stay SILENT, because the offer
' costs a terminal: not the sbx backend, not a subscription harness, a usable
' file login, something already in sbx's store, or an env credential carrying
' the session — any one of those and proveo says nothing.
'
' ── THE ONE REFUSAL, AND THE ONE THAT WAS REMOVED ──────────────────────────
' A subscription harness with NO credential at all is refused on this backend
' before launch. The agent cannot complete a login here: it reaches the prompt,
' exits, and the sandbox stops with it — which surfaces ~30s later as an unrelated
' 137. Refusing costs nothing; launching costs a minute of image load to reach a
' failure that was knowable up front. The refusal is gated on the persisted login
' as well as on MissingEnv, or it would refuse runs whose credential is already in
' the proveo home, and it prints `claude setup-token` plus the operator's own
' shell's export line rather than a bare error.
'
' There is deliberately NO stale-login refusal any more. It was added when the
' mounted proveo home WAS this backend's credential, so a login that could not
' renew meant a run that could not authenticate. That stopped being true when the
' HOME redirect went (sandbox.Home): sbx runs its own agent user and its credential
' proxy writes the live credential into that user's home, so the file under the
' proveo home is not consulted and its freshness decides nothing. Refusing on it
' would block runs that WORK — verified by e2e/ladder_test.go, whose rung 3 carries
' this exact Kit.
'
' ── AN UNVERIFIED FINDING, RECORDED RATHER THAN ACTED ON ───────────────────
' proveo writes `sbx secret set --force <ENV_VAR_NAME>` — ANTHROPIC_API_KEY,
' CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, CURSOR_API_KEY. sbx keys injection on SERVICE names
' (anthropic, openai, …), and upstream says "Built-in agents declare a fixed
' set of services. Custom kits can declare their own." proveo's Kit
' deliberately declares none (credential-path.puml), so those entries match no
' declaration and are probably never injected.
'
' Three things are measured, one is not:
'   MEASURED  sbx validates nothing. `sbx secret set proveo-probe-not-a-service`
'             stored happily, listed as TYPE "service".
'   MEASURED  the built-in anthropic service declares apiKey.name
'             ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — the env var is the FIELD, not the key.
'   MEASURED  `set-custom` exists for credentials that "don't fit the
'             service-identifier model", which is what an arbitrary name is.
'   NOT MEASURED  whether the proxy injects such an entry. Proving it needs a
'             run with the store holding only the mis-named entry, and this
'             host's store also holds a working `anthropic` oauth credential —
'             so the isolating test means removing the operator's own entry.
'
' NOT FIXED, deliberately. Correcting the name would make proveo's writes take
' EFFECT, and upstream says "the API key takes precedence and OAuth acts as the
' fallback" — so a correctly-named anthropic API key would displace a working
' subscription login and bill per token. That is the exact failure
' credential-path.puml already records. The fix belongs behind the auth row,
' not in a rename.
title sbx OAuth provisioning — one writer, and it is not proveo

actor "operator" as OP

node "host" as HOST <<host>> {
  database "login Keychain\n""Claude Code-credentials"" — the whole blob" as KC <<db>>
  component "proveo\nreads it, reports it, drops it" as PV <<app>>
  database "sbx's own store\ncom.docker.sandboxes — the SAME keychain" as SBXSTORE <<db>>
  component "sbx proxy\nholds the tokens, owns the refresh" as PROXY <<app>>
}

node "sandbox" as BOX <<app>> {
  component "claude auth login\nthe one writer sbx recognises" as LOGIN <<app>>
  component "the agent\nsees sentinels, never a token" as AGENT <<app>>
}

component "an API key written under an ENV VAR name\nmatches no service declaration" as MISNAMED <<error>>
component "writing sbx's keychain entry directly\nundocumented, breaks on the next release" as DIRECT <<error>>

KC ARROW_MAIN PV : read at RUN TIME
PV ARROW_OPTIONAL OP : names what exists, and the argv that fixes it
OP ARROW_MAIN LOGIN : ""sbx run claude <ws> -- auth login""
LOGIN ARROW_MAIN PROXY : the proxy captures the exchange
PROXY ARROW_MAIN SBXSTORE : stored host-side
SBXSTORE ARROW_MAIN PROXY : read per request
PROXY ARROW_MAIN AGENT : sentinels in, real tokens never
PV ARROW_ERROR SBXSTORE : no import path — upstream, verbatim
PV ARROW_ERROR MISNAMED : what proveo writes today
PV ARROW_ERROR DIRECT : refused

note bottom of KC
  **The right unit, the wrong destination.**
  The blob is the whole credential — both tokens,
  both stamps — which is exactly what sbx's oauth
  slot holds. There is simply no way to put it
  there.
end note

note bottom of PV
  **It renders the argv, never runs it.** The
  sign-in needs a browser and a terminal, so a
  run that started one would hang waiting for
  somebody who may not be there.
end note

note top of PV
  **And it says nothing five ways.** Not sbx, not
  a subscription harness, a usable file login,
  something already stored, or an env credential
  carrying the session — any one and it is quiet.
end note

note bottom of SBXSTORE
  **The same keychain, a different owner.** On
  darwin sbx stores its secrets in the login
  Keychain too. Only ITS entry is wired to the
  proxy, which is why the credential has to
  arrive through the sign-in.
end note

note bottom of LOGIN
  **No credential at all is refused up front.**
  The agent reaches its login prompt, exits, and
  the sandbox stops with it — surfacing ~30s
  later as an unrelated 137, after a minute of
  image load nobody needed to pay.
end note

note top of LOGIN
  **A STALE login is not refused, deliberately.**
  Since the HOME redirect went, sbx's proxy owns
  the live credential and the file under the
  proveo home decides nothing. Refusing on it
  blocked runs that work (ladder rung 3).
end note

note bottom of MISNAMED
  **Recorded, not fixed.** Renaming it would make
  the write take effect — and an API key
  outranks OAuth upstream, so a working
  subscription would start billing per token.
  The fix belongs behind the auth row.
end note

note bottom of DIRECT
  **The one option that looks like a shortcut.**
  Reverse-engineering ""docker/sandbox/default/
  anthropic"" would fail silently the first time
  sbx changed its storage shape, and fail on the
  operator's next run rather than ours.
end note
@enduml
